Fault Tolerant Vault environment:-
Each retry_join stanza can include DNS names or IP addresses and the port
Manually join: vault operator raft join https://active_node.example.com:8200
If auto unseal use -> On leader run (vault operator init, vault operator unseal) -> on other follower node run the "vault operator raft join https://active_node.example.com:8200" command to join this node to Vault cluster
If manual unseal keys used then -> On leader run (vault operator init, vault operator unseal) -> on other follower node run the "vault operator raft join https://active_node.example.com:8200" command to join this node to Vault cluster -> manually unseal this node using unseal keys

DR Replication:-
The primary cluster acts as the system of record and replicates most Vault data asynchronously
Cannot service reads from client requests
Sort of warm standby cluster
Will replicate tokens and leases created on the primary cluster
If Vault sits behind a load balancer which is terminating TLS, it will break the mutual TLS between the nodes if inter-cluster traffic is forced through the load balancer.
The secondary token is protected with response wrapping. Once the token is successfully used, it is useless (single-use token). 
The secondary token includes information such as:
• The redirect address of the primary cluster
• The client certificate and CA certificate
                        1. Activate DR Replication
                        primary$ vault write -f sys/replication/dr/primary/enable
                        2. Create the Secondary Token
                        primary$ vault write sys/replication/dr/primary/secondary-token id=<id>
                        3. Activate the Secondary Cluster
                        secondary$ vault write sys/replication/dr/secondary/enable token=<token>
Promote DR secondary to primary (in case primary gets down):-
Promotion of a DR cluster requires a DR Operation Token
This is generated directly on the DR cluster using the unseal/recovery keys. Process is similar to generating a root token (requires threshold of keys).
How to generate DR Operation token?
Initialize The Process: vault operator generate-root -dr-token –init   (you get otp from this command)
Provide the Keys: vault operator generate-root -dr-token (you will enter 3 keys here and then you get encoded token)
Decode the DR operation token: 
            vault operator generate-root -dr-token /
            -decode="LgQCHzFBByxxxxxxxxIyF1dA" /
            -otp="Frqxxxxxxxxxx46"
Promote the cluster: 
            vault write sys/replication/dr/secondary/promote /
            dr_operation_token=hvxxxxxxxxxxLAB

Vault Agent:
To help secure tokens when using Auth-Auth, you can have Vault response wrap the token when the Vault Agent authenticates
• Response wrapped by the auth method
• Response wrapped by the token sink
The placement of the wrap_ttl in the Vault Agent configuration file determines where the response wrapping happens.


                        

            

